The provided inOrder function performs an in-order traversal of a binary tree without using recursion or an explicit stack. Instead, it employs the Morris Traversal algorithm, which modifies the tree's structure temporarily to achieve O(1) space complexity. 
The function returns a vector containing the values of the nodes in in-order sequence.

How It Works:
Initialization:
An empty vector ans is created to store the in-order traversal result.
The traversal starts from the root node.
Traversal Loop:
The algorithm iterates while the current root is not NULL.
Case 1: No Left Child
If the current root node does not have a left child:
Visit the node: Add root->data to the ans vector.
Move to Right: Update root to its right child (root = root->right).
Case 2: Node with Left Child
If the current root node has a left child:
Find Predecessor: Locate the rightmost node (curr) in the left subtree of root. This node is the in-order predecessor of root.
Thread Creation:
If curr->right is NULL, set curr->right to point back to root (creating a temporary thread) and move root to its left child.
Thread Removal & Visit:
If curr->right already points to root, it means the left subtree has been fully traversed. Remove the temporary thread (curr->right = NULL), add root->data to ans, and move root to its right child.
Completion:
The loop continues until all nodes are visited.
Finally, the ans vector containing the in-order traversal is returned.
Example Usage:
Suppose you have the following binary tree:

TC: O(n)
SC: O(1)

//CODE
vector<int> inOrder(Node* root)
{
    vector<int> ans;
    while(root){
        if(!root->left){
            ans.push_back(root->data);
            root = root->right;
        }
        else{
            Node* curr = root->left;
            while(curr->right && curr->right != root){
                curr = curr->right;
            }
            if(!curr->right){
                curr->right = root;
                root = root->left;
            }
            else{
                ans.push_back(root->data);
                curr->right = NULL;
                root = root->right;
            }
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
This function effectively traverses the binary tree in in-order without using additional memory for recursion or stack, making it an efficient solution for certain applications.
